{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply skeptical of reckless military campaigns against Muslim forces. He became known for his pragmatic diplomacy and his willingness to negotiate with Saladin rather than engage in constant warfare.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond assumed power while still young yet his reign began under difficult circumstances since his father died in battle. The young count faced immediate threats from neighboring powers and he struggled to maintain stability within his own domains. His capture by Nur ad-Din Zengi marked a turning point because it exposed him to Islamic culture and politics firsthand. This experience taught him the value of patience and strategic alliances over brute force. Upon release he returned to Tripoli with a new perspective that often clashed with more aggressive factions in the Latin East.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe later acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule independently. Raymond sought to protect the kingdom from internal discord and external invasion by promoting peace treaties. His leadership style caused friction with the Knights Templar and other military orders who preferred offensive actions against Muslim territories. These groups viewed his caution as weakness or even treason despite his efforts to preserve Christian holdings through negotiation. The political landscape remained volatile as rival noble families vied for influence over the ailing king.\n\n*Legacy and Historical Impact*\nRaymond I